WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:03.280
I saw someone die late last night

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.920
Have you been taking your medication

00:00:12.639 --> 00:00:14.559
You don't get it

00:00:14.559 --> 00:00:16.559
She's here

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.680
She followed me here

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.160
You're not in your right state of mind

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:24.160
Look

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.280
Yesterday we just dropped the short film "You Monster" on the Corridor channel

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:45.660
Now this piece explores how audiences affect creators and we're gonna sit down with Anthony Padilla, who plays the lead character Ashton

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.980
Now he's a veteran of the Youtube game. He's had an audience for years

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.540
We're gonna see how the themes of this piece resonate with his personal life and experiences

00:00:54.559 --> 00:01:00.479
What we shot this piece back in November and then uh, there's been a worldwide pandemic and a large

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.020
Uh movement against police brutality since then

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:07.480
Has it ever felt appropriate to release this. I mean even now it's like

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.840
Damn, the world is just very different than it was eight months ago when we shot it

00:01:12.159 --> 00:01:14.798
Yeah, you know, but it's one of those things where I don't want to keep sitting on it

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:19.359
I just want to put it out there because you know the question this piece asks is how does an audience change things?

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:23.679
You know sometimes for the worse sometimes for the better, you know, you're somebody who's had an audience

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.759
You've had an audience for a long time. I mean you're you're a veteran of of the industry at this point

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.640
You took part this project. You know you brought your acting talents to the table

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:34.959
Obviously we're friends, we go back a ways, but was there anything that spoke to you personally in this?

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.179
Is there anything that like stood out as a message that you that made you interested?

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.880
Yeah, I mean I think the whole

00:01:41.180 --> 00:01:45.680
Concept of an audience wanting you to document things

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.440
and people who, you know, get obsessed with these numbers and engagement

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:59.119
It becomes very easy as someone who does social media to start gravitating toward the things that get you the most

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.600
engagement, the most viewership because that translates to

00:02:04.159 --> 00:02:07.679
Success in all the different ways. I feel like a lot of people get in their head

00:02:07.759 --> 00:02:12.079
I'll use these cheap tactics whenever I can, because it'll allow me to do more things

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.520
It'll unlock more doors for me in the future

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:17.240
I don't think it's necessarily malicious

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:22.080
I don't think there's this intent to do things that are inherently evil, but I think some people get

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:25.120
Just blinded by

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.679
The opportunities that success can open up

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.000
Have you ever found yourself doing something or not doing something specifically because you have an audience

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:35.920
I think there was a time

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.959
You know when, when things started becoming more what some people call PC culture?

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:46.160
And there was a time where I made really edgy jokes because I thought they were funny because they were shocking

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.959
Um, and there was, I think there was a brief moment where I didn't understand

00:02:51.599 --> 00:02:56.719
reasons for why some things weren't appropriate, but I feel like I was able to

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:03.840
Learn why it affected people and in turn kind of become more empathetic with why people, you know, found certain things

00:03:04.459 --> 00:03:06.459
offensive or

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.560
Why people should or shouldn't say certain things.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:11.800
So, I feel like over the years I've, I've started to

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:15.800
understand and learn more why things affect people in different ways

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.780
So, I don't find myself having to hold back at all because I actually my mentality

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:21.140
my view of

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:25.440
The things going on in the world has just changed so drastically since that time

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:28.799
When you can work that stuff into your core and just be who you are

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:30.560
Naturally, that's you know

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.399
That's the best state you can possibly be in, you know, with the role of the audience this video

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:38.560
Obviously, it casts a negative light on how people can influence. Uh

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.599
And encourage people to do things that they otherwise wouldn't do, but you know, it's not all negatives, you know

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.960
Do you have any examples of positives where you think, you know an audience putting attention onto something has has made things better

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:54.580
Yeah, I mean, I think, I think even right now with the Black Lives Matter movement with so much focus on police brutality

00:03:54.959 --> 00:04:02.158
And systemic racism, and I think it's really had a positive effect because people have seen so much encouragement from their audience

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:08.720
And just the media in general to, to speak up about these things. So I think there has been some positivity

00:04:08.720 --> 00:04:13.039
Yeah, there's also been negative too, but I think in many ways there has been a ton of positive

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:17.900
What, what responsibility do you think an audience has, uh, to the content they consume?

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:24.060
It's funny you ask that, the audience is in some ways directly responsible for what kind of content gets made

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.720
Because you're voting with your view, if you want more content like that to be made. If you're someone who hate watches things

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.079
You know, you're like, I hate this so much, but I love how much I hate it

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:36.580
I love talking with my friends about how much I hate it.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.280
You're voting for more content like that to be made

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.400
It's half the person who creates the content and then it's half

00:04:42.639 --> 00:04:45.839
The audience telling that creator that they want more of that to be made

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.160
And then with their view also giving them the money to fund doing more of that as well

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.320
It's a really good insight on it I have to agree with you

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:55.680
It's definitely, it's like voting with your dollar, except you're voting with your view

00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:58.160
You know, people... people feel like there's a million people watching video

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:03.780
Why would your view impact it, but at the same time I see so many reminders of how small the world is

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:07.520
That one view does make a difference, that one comment you make does make a difference

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.820
It's small, but at the same time it's not small

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.720
The moment you get 5, 10, 20, 100 people saying the same thing

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:20.960
100 comments on a Youtube video, even if it's a super popular one, all saying the same thing, like at least for me, I noticed that creators take notice to that

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:22.560
Yeah, yeah

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.400
And I would say to people out there

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:28.880
Don't get discouraged by how big the numbers are, keep in mind that an individual is still very powerful

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:29.920
Absolutely

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:37.120
Every once in a while there's a single comment that gives me some insight into something, either that i'm going to do, or something that I have done that

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:43.440
Completely shifts my, my perception of the way that I would go about doing that and I think it's super important

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.520
Everyone should be speaking up more if they have something to say

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:51.200
I think that, you know, at least I think you can speak to and I can speak to, is you do get a chance

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:56.880
As a person with an audience to refine yourself for the better, to polish off those rough edges

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:01.440
And get a greater understanding of people across all different walks of life and how things work

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:05.280
And I think that's a responsibility as a creator, that you bring to the table when you have an audience

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:09.360
You know, that it's important that you grow with that audience to not only learn from the audience

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:13.679
But then take that learning from the audience and give it back to them, that they can learn as well

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.380
You know, it's all just one big circle of education

00:06:16.500 --> 00:06:17.480
Yeah, absolutely

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:22.640
I say like, you know, look back at content that you used to make 10 or 15 years ago or even imagine that you

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.720
Are who you were 10 or 15 years ago and look at the content that you make now and you could just see how much

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:32.319
Growth there has been and i'm sure a huge part of that has been interacting with your audience and learning from them

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:35.920
And then in turn kind of shaping your view of the world at the same time

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:36.780
Yeah

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:38.320
So to tie this back to "You Monster"

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:40.480
When do you film and when do you help?

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:43.560
When does it become not okay, to continue having your phone out?

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:44.840
And when do you get involved?

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.780
If you were in a situation, let's say, you were witnessing a fight

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:49.520
When do you think it's okay to be recording it?

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:51.980
And when do you think a line gets crossed?

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:55.480
And you should either no longer be recording it, or you should step in, to do something about it

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.780
It's best to quickly assess the situation

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:58.540
"Am I safe?"

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.280
"Would it be safe for me to step in?"

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.460
And if the answer is, "Yes, It would be safe"

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.180
Then you should definitely go and help

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:07.980
Like in "You Monster", my character should have definitely helped

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:10.100
There was nothing that would have hurt me

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:15.360
In going up and like, helping that person or talking to them or trying to understand what was going on in their mind

00:07:15.759 --> 00:07:22.639
But, you know, say it's an instance where there's some kind of violence going on or obviously police brutality, you know

00:07:22.639 --> 00:07:28.079
I think if you can't directly contribute because it might be putting your life in danger in some way

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:33.840
It would probably be best to record that, so you could show people and talk about it and make it a bigger thing

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:36.960
With George Floyd in that unfortunate situation

00:07:37.039 --> 00:07:39.279
They, they didn't feel safe stepping in

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:43.280
So, I think they did the best that they could do and that was document it

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:48.800
So that it could spark a discussion and in turn there's been an entire worldwide movement because of that

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.100
So I think those people did the best that they could do with the situation that they were in

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.020
Yeah I think it's great insight and, uh, good advice also

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:59.240
You know, I want to say thank you again, for getting involved in this project

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:05.120
For lending, you know, your... your talents on screen and taking the time to nail the character and help us make a piece

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:07.820
That's a little bit more than just a VFX action scene

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:09.780
Yeah, absolutely, I had a great time

00:08:09.780 --> 00:08:13.980
I mean, as a director you gave a lot of freedom to, uh, improvise

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:20.420
It just, you pulled something, that feels so big together with such a small crew and small budget

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:23.120
And uh, i'm really, really happy with how it turned out

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:24.720
So, thank you for having me be part of that

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:25.480
You bet man

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:27.800
You know, thanks for taking a minute of your day here to talk to us

00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:30.880
And uh, I look forward to getting that video out there and seeing what people think

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:32.320
Absolutely, man, thank you

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:35.820
And uh, you know, thanks to the whole production team, it was a really fun time

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.039
I'd love to hear your thoughts

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:41.999
I'd love to hear if you think audiences bear any responsibility in the content that is created for them

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.479
And if so, what kind of responsibility do they bear?

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:47.919
Have you ever personally been affected by having an audience?

00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:52.000
You know, I know not everybody out here has, you know, say Youtube channels or Instagram followings

00:08:52.120 --> 00:08:53.520
But even small followings

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.160
Has that shaped or changed the way you approach life?

00:08:56.160 --> 00:08:58.480
Have you ever been recording a video and been thinking to yourself

00:08:58.620 --> 00:09:03.180
"I maybe should put my phone away and get involved in some other way, than just filming it"

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:04.340
Let me know in the comments below

00:09:04.340 --> 00:09:05.540
I'd love to hear your stories

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:06.560
I'd love to hear your thoughts

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:08.780
I'd love for you to go check out "You Monster"

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:10.720
It's a piece that we put a lot of work into

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:12.220
It's an idea I've had for a long time

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:14.400
And you should also go check out Anthony's channel

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.680
He does some awesome eye-opening sit-downs where he talks to really interesting people

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:20.980
So we'll have links for his stuff as well

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:21.920
All right

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:22.640
Happy watching

